/**

\page MapperRegistrationMigration Migration Guide from CoreObjectFactory to Mapper Provider Services

\tableofcontents

mitk::CoreObjectFactory and mitk::CoreObjectFactoryBase have been removed.
Their last remaining responsibilities, creating mappers for data nodes and
applying default rendering properties, moved to mitk::IMapperProvider micro
services, mirroring how file readers and writers are registered. See
\ref QVTKRendering_registerMapper for a description of the new mechanism.

\section MapperRegistrationMigrationHowto How to adapt your code

Replace your mitk::CoreObjectFactoryBase subclass and its file-scope static
registration with one mitk::MapperProviderBase instance per mapper class and
mapper slot, held by your module activator.

\code
// Old: object factory subclass with type dispatch and static registration
class ExampleObjectFactory : public mitk::CoreObjectFactoryBase
{
public:
  mitk::Mapper::Pointer CreateMapper(mitk::DataNode* node, MapperSlotId slotId) override
  {
    mitk::Mapper::Pointer mapper;

    if (dynamic_cast<ExampleData*>(node->GetData()) != nullptr)
    {
      if (slotId == mitk::BaseRenderer::Standard2D)
        mapper = ExampleMapper2D::New();
      else if (slotId == mitk::BaseRenderer::Standard3D)
        mapper = ExampleMapper3D::New();

      if (mapper.IsNotNull())
        mapper->SetDataNode(node);
    }

    return mapper;
  }

  void SetDefaultProperties(mitk::DataNode* node) override
  {
    if (dynamic_cast<ExampleData*>(node->GetData()) != nullptr)
    {
      ExampleMapper2D::SetDefaultProperties(node);
      ExampleMapper3D::SetDefaultProperties(node);
    }
  }
};

struct RegisterExampleObjectFactory
{
  RegisterExampleObjectFactory() : m_Factory(ExampleObjectFactory::New())
  {
    mitk::CoreObjectFactory::GetInstance()->RegisterExtraFactory(m_Factory);
  }

  ~RegisterExampleObjectFactory()
  {
    mitk::CoreObjectFactory::GetInstance()->UnRegisterExtraFactory(m_Factory);
  }

  ExampleObjectFactory::Pointer m_Factory;
};

static RegisterExampleObjectFactory registerExampleObjectFactory;
\endcode

\code
// New: mapper providers registered in the module activator
#include <mitkMapperProviderBase.h>

class ExampleModuleActivator : public us::ModuleActivator
{
public:
  void Load(us::ModuleContext*) override
  {
    m_MapperProviders.push_back(
      std::make_unique<mitk::MapperProviderBase<ExampleMapper2D, ExampleData>>(
        mitk::BaseRenderer::Standard2D));
    m_MapperProviders.push_back(
      std::make_unique<mitk::MapperProviderBase<ExampleMapper3D, ExampleData>>(
        mitk::BaseRenderer::Standard3D));
  }

  void Unload(us::ModuleContext*) override
  {
    m_MapperProviders.clear();
  }

private:
  std::vector<std::unique_ptr<mitk::IMapperProvider>> m_MapperProviders;
};

US_EXPORT_MODULE_ACTIVATOR(ExampleModuleActivator)
\endcode

The type dispatch disappears: the targeted data class and slot are service
properties, and the mappers' static SetDefaultProperties() is called through
the provider. If your module already has an activator, add the registrations
there.

\section MapperRegistrationMigrationDetails Behavioral differences to be aware of

<ul>
  <li> Provider selection is deterministic. It walks the class hierarchy of a
       node's data from the most derived class to the base class; a provider
       registered for a subclass always wins over one registered for a base
       class. Previously, extra factories were consulted in arbitrary order
       and always won against the built-in factory.
  <li> To replace a mapper that MITK registers by default, register your
       provider for the same data class with a service ranking greater
       than zero (third constructor argument of mitk::MapperProviderBase).
       Relying on "extra factories always win" no longer works.
  <li> A factory that matched nodes by criteria other than the data type must
       become a mitk::MapperProviderBase subclass that overrides
       CreateMapper() and returns nullptr to decline a node; selection then
       falls through to the next candidate or hierarchy level.
  <li> Default properties are applied most derived data class first. Since
       AddProperty() does not overwrite existing values by default, the most
       specific mapper determines conflicting property values. Previously,
       built-in defaults ran before extra-factory defaults.
</ul>

*/
